+# Invocation and artifact ownership
+
+A terminal conversation and a headless conversation use the same generation,
+execution, and retention contracts. Renderers project facts produced by those
+contracts. Generation logic does not select a consumer mode.
+
+## Generation
+
+An invocation owns its cache scope independently of the model serving it. The
+main conversation owns its session identity. A child launched by a tool owns the
+spawning tool-call identity, including when it resumes from a persisted record.
+A workflow call or utility fork owns its own invocation identity. Changing a
+model does not change this ownership.
+
+`GenerationContext` is immutable. A chat and an agent runtime require one at
+construction. The context binds a provider through `GenerationClient`, which
+constructs owned requests for batch generation, streamed generation, and exact
+request counting. The raw provider interface requires that context. The final
+wire request takes `kv_scope` from it after provider decoration; model settings
+and extra request fields cannot replace it.
+
+Every generation and exact-count request also carries its own nonempty model.
+The common context constructor rejects missing or malformed models before
+provider hooks or conversion; configured defaults cannot replace that identity.
+Stream guards resolve explicit configuration, then environment, then an absent
+setting's default. Selected values must be integral milliseconds from zero
+through the JavaScript timer ceiling. Zero disables a guard. Malformed supplied
+settings reject construction before the provider client is created.
+
+Async-local invocation frames let tools and hooks holding a shared Config access
+the actual child's owner. The frame covers launch preparation and initial hooks,
+execution, and continuation. A chat also retains its own context, so later calls
+from a different ambient frame cannot reassign it. Parent work which is triggered
+from a child event exits the child execution frame explicitly.
+
+A configured generation route must use the OpenAI-compatible vLLM contract,
+strict tool-call commitment, and an explicit positive integer context window.
+The tokenizer URL is derived by the same function during configuration and
+request sizing. Unsupported selections fail before activation; failed model
+switches restore the previous selection. Missing requested routes and provider
+construction failures fail the request. Forks and side queries resolve routes
+through the same resolver.
+
+Provider activation publishes the provider and its configuration as one prepared
+pair. Status reads may observe that no pair is ready. Generation and operations
+requiring active provider facts pass through one readiness guard. Utility clients
+retain Config rather than another provider instance; generation and embeddings
+resolve the active invocation's pair, including when entering and leaving child
+frames. A cached utility client cannot bypass readiness.
+
+Request preparation ends with an exact count of the rendered request after
+compaction. The configured session-token limit applies to that final count;
+refusal carries the count and limit as named error properties. An accepted
+compaction is already durable, so its attempt and replacement events precede
+any later refusal. Request admission, recorded dispatch intent, and a received
+response are separate facts. The internal admitted event establishes only that
+preparation succeeded.
+
+ACP owns preparation, stream consumption, and settlement in one method used by
+ordinary prompts, automatic continuations, scheduled work, and notifications.
+Callers render ordinary events and handle their own terminal response. The common
+owner projects compaction notices and classifies exact token refusals only after
+settlement. Stopped sends report whether the input is already retained by the
+chat, preventing duplicate preservation after an accepted compaction.
+
+## Tool artifacts
+
+Large text results and downloaded binary content use a single session artifact
+store in persistent project storage. The directory is keyed by a hash of the
+owning session identity. Each payload is named by its content hash, created
+exclusively, made read-only, and synced together with its directory ancestry.
+Equal payloads reuse the same immutable file only after verifying the bytes.
+
+The quota counts the actual retained files while holding a cross-process writer
+lock. Recreating Config does not reset it. Partial writes from interrupted
+writers continue to occupy quota. The lock is never taken from another writer
+based on elapsed time; an abandoned writer lock requires explicit administrative
+recovery. This deliberately refuses writes when exclusive ownership cannot be
+established.
+
+References remain readable across session recreation, resume, and elapsed time.
+There is no automatic age-based collection. Forks and exported transcripts can
+retain references after the originating chat is deleted, so deleting a chat does
+not authorize deleting its artifacts. Disposal requires explicitly disposing of
+all references to the owning artifact directory.
+
+## Approval provenance and text fidelity
+
+Model arguments satisfy the advertised tool schema as written. External schema
+openness and pattern properties retain their JSON Schema meaning. Native closed
+schemas remain closed. Trusted editor modifications are invocation metadata held
+outside model JSON; the scheduler's parameter clone carries this authenticated
+metadata. Serialized lookalike parameters cannot acquire it.
+
+Structured tool calls determine executable actions. Assistant text remains
+verbatim even when it resembles protocol XML. An absent provider terminal reason
+remains absent through conversion and is shown as incomplete output. Human and
+machine renderers observe the same completion fact.
+
+## Recording and session transitions
+
+Config acquires the same session writer lease before activating any recording
+session. The recorder captures its storage anchor and refuses writes before
+activation or after a durable failure. Changing the execution directory cannot
+move the transcript, lock, artifact directory, or usage ownership.
+
+Session replacement is one asynchronous operation: drain and close the outgoing
+owner, acquire and reload the incoming transcript, restore the model history, and
+publish the new session. Failed replacement restores the outgoing owner and
+history. A failed rollback refuses generation. UI lists change after the owned
+transition succeeds.
+
+Every GeminiChat requires a canonical commit recorder. Root recording and the
+existing child writer implement that obligation for ordinary, resumed,
+background, workflow, utility, and speculative chats. A display event cannot
+acknowledge a durable write. Full bootstrap and adopted history retain authored
+parts. An assistant record is durable before its structured calls become live.
+Failure diagnostics retain partial text, the last valid served report, and the
+provider terminal or its absence. A failed Goal attempt retains a copied permit
+in top-level system-record metadata, preserving turn lineage without admitting
+partial output as delivered Goal evidence.
+Goal reads and clears obtain the selected session's recovered runtime. An empty
+snapshot describes an established empty state; unavailable persistence or failed
+recovery rejects the operation in both command and ACP consumers.
+
+Session mutations share the writer lease. An active-source fork waits behind the
+recorder queue and copies a strict snapshot. Target failures and source integrity
+failures have different owners. Rename, archive, deletion, and their cleanup
+await the owning operation; retained usage is durable before transcript deletion.
+The retention writer creates its independent storage directory before acquiring
+the append lock. Its existing directory sync publishes the record and every
+ancestor before retention succeeds. A retention or deletion-cleanup failure
+propagates through the mutation owner instead of being relabeled as a missing
+transcript.
+Maintenance holds the lease through its target-location read and scheduled-task
+update, including requests repeated after partial failure and deletion of an
+already absent transcript. It returns a result only after release. Error
+observers run once and cannot replace the operation failure. REST and ACP use
+one error serializer, retaining the same constituent failure messages.
+
+A workflow dispatch owns one execution. Its inactivity watchdog cancels stalled
+work and joins its cleanup; it cannot replay a task whose earlier turns may
+have performed effects. Original and cleanup failures remain observable.
+
+## Accounting and authored input
+
+A durable dispatch identifies an attempted request before provider admission.
+Its final observation has the same request identity and either a complete served
+usage report or explicit missing usage. Missing finalization is an independent
+fact. One observation accumulator supplies live, recorded, workflow, child, and
+export summaries. Its counters distinguish reported zero, no requests, missing
+usage, and unfinalized observations.
+
+Reported prompt, output, reasoning, cached prompt, and total counts are safe
+nonnegative integers. Total is prompt plus output; reasoning is included in
+output and cached prompt is included in prompt. Exact rendered-request sizing
+is separate from the last provider-served prompt. A context display cannot use
+one as a substitute for the other.
+
+Recorded replay returns the same dispatch/finalization observations as live accounting.
+The review ledger and transcript export use the common generation summary, including
+safe aggregate totals and explicit missing reports. Their renderers retain each
+invocation owner; display labels do not create a second accounting owner.
+Live session, model and source metrics project the original observations through
+that same summary. Retained summaries combine through its validated addition.
+Mutable timing, error and tool activity remain separate facts. Renderers consume
+complete snapshots and never reconstruct token totals from display buckets.
+Daily dashboard and terminal history views share the same grouping operation.
+Missing served counts and undefined rates remain unavailable in charts, tables,
+tooltips, and exports; a reported zero remains visible. Root wire results carry
+the same observation summary. Per-assistant served reports and absent child
+aggregate reports retain their separate meanings.
+
+Todo plans carry status and identity. Details show observed start and completion
+times, preserving a paused interval and clearing it when a completed task reopens.
+No request or tool resource total is inferred from the times at which a todo changed.
+
+Replay cursors and bridge initialization use one versioned state parser. Invalid
+pending entries, duplicate call IDs, missing versions, and invalid supplied Goal
+facts refuse continuation. The canonical pending-tool shape carries source identity
+through full and paged replay, with no intermediate translation.
+
+Original authored inputs carry provenance captured before transformations.
+Compaction retains those parts outside the generated snapshot, so repeated
+summaries do not own their verbatim survival. Model summaries must satisfy the
+six-section structural contract and exact request-size constraints. Structural
+validation does not certify semantic correctness of the model's summary.
+
+## Output and shutdown
+
+Each writable stream has one callback-tracking output writer shared by its
+adapters. Flush waits for the actual pending callbacks, including writes for
+which Node returned true. A terminal result is acknowledged only after recording
+and output settle. Queued turns retain their delivered-result state through
+cleanup; a cleanup failure after delivery is diagnostic and cannot mint a second
+terminal result.
+
+The shared cleanup owner joins every admitted obligation, preserves reported
+independent errors, and does
+not abandon work at an arbitrary deadline. Bridges flush before closing their
+stream. Terminal rendering unmounts and restores each terminal facility even if
+another cleanup fails. Fatal queued work cancels input admission, including an
+open pipe and lines already buffered by readline.
+
+Captured wire JSONL promises complete LF-terminated records on orderly exit. An
+abrupt process or transport failure can leave an incomplete final record. The
+harness scans one descriptor snapshot for owned observations and independent
+complete certification. Earlier valid observations survive corruption; every
+unaccounted record remains explicit. Partial observations never become a
+certified complete result. Canonical history readers remain strict because
+resuming a partially recovered instruction history is a different obligation.
+
+Compaction and child reasoning remain inline. A complete reference design needs
+an explicit stored-content representation, durable encoding through both root
+and child writers, strict resolution for model history and export, and reference
+projections for daemon and SDK readers. Artifact retention and output settlement
+are prerequisites; they do not constitute that unimplemented change.
+
+Speculation acceptance has a separate unresolved transaction boundary. Overlay
+files are applied before adopted messages are appended, one message at a time.
+An append failure can therefore leave changed files and a partially adopted
+history. The overlay also suppresses individual copy and removal failures. The
+caller reports acceptance failure and preserves cleanup errors; it does not
+resubmit the prompt. Atomic acceptance requires coordinated file/history
+ownership and recoverable publication, including explicit per-file failures.
+
+Review worktree ownership and cleanup are deliberately excluded because this
+project does not use fetch-pr or PR worktrees. They remain unresolved. Per-PR lease publication
+can overwrite another invocation's lease; creation removes fixed worktree and
+branch paths without establishing that their prior owner has ended. Separate
+cleanup implementations suppress read and removal errors and may force filesystem
+removal after Git refusal. The shared cleanup owner cannot preserve failures
+suppressed by these callees. Correctness requires exclusive invocation ownership
+across review, probe and base worktrees, branches and associated files, shared by
+creation and both cleanup entry points.
+
+Operational daemon metrics remain incorrect. The ring admits malformed or partial
+token observations, permits unsafe addition, and exposes mutable sampled buckets.
+The bridge suppresses accounting callback failures and fills missing counts with
+zero; the sampler also fills failed or absent readings with zero. A complete fix
+must coordinate observation admission, per-frame mutation, measurement failure
+and staleness, checked interval arithmetic, and immutable publication. Sampled
+operational readings are distinct from complete session generation accounting.
+That whole lifecycle remains open; the session summary corrections do not close
+it. Fresh CPU-only tests reproduce all ten ring defects. Source review also
+confirms that UI retention drops closed-session observations, nested observation
+objects remain mutable, and optional telemetry catches can suppress admission
+failures. A whole repair needs source-owned observation retention, acknowledged
+channel publication and explicit resource measurement states. CPU qualification
+is feasible; the unimplemented coordinated protocol is the remaining limit.
+
+## Verification scope
+
+Tests exercise the source with mocked provider boundaries and temporary local
+files. They cover overlapping owners, initial and resumed child hooks, exact
+wire ownership after decoration, unsupported model selection and rollback,
+concurrent artifact writers and quota contention, retained references, empty
+editor approvals, external schema openness, and missing provider terminals.
+Application builds and live provider or CLI sessions are separate deployment
+verification and are not prerequisites for these source tests.
+
+Prompt-hook failure policy remains open. Invalid model JSON/schema and operational
+failures currently allow continuation. Cancellation is inferred from message
+text, and the enclosing hook event bus reports failure without a blocking output
+for most event kinds. A correct policy must distinguish failed evaluation from a
+model decision and respect whether an event occurs before admission or after an
+irreversible action. The shared generation owner does not repair that policy.
+These paths are source-confirmed; existing fixtures preserve the current behavior,
+not a qualification of fail-closed operation.
+
+Resident background AgentTool disposal still exposes a void callback, and its
+unexpected asynchronous failures are logged without an owning caller joining
+them. Writer attachment is shared by all children, foreground forks are awaited,
+and explicit background forks use the same lifetime for every renderer. Ordinary
+child resource settlement and InProcessBackend settlement are awaited. The
+remaining resident-background disposal contract needs coordinated changes to the
+registry, continuation, caller shutdown, and failure publication; it is not closed
+by those narrower lifetime fixes.
+
+Provider-stream lifetime and failure settlement remain open. The pipeline acquires
+an SDK stream before returning lazy generators, so returning before their first
+iteration can bypass their cleanup. The guarded iterator suppresses every
+`return()` failure, and automatic iterator closing after a conversion failure
+can hide an independent cleanup failure. Guards disabled and guards enabled also
+follow different iterator ownership paths. A complete fix must own the stream
+from acquisition through completion, abandonment, cancellation, and pending-read
+settlement, preserving primary and cleanup failures. Strict configuration
+admission does not establish that lifetime contract. CPU-only tests using the
+installed OpenAI SDK and in-memory SSE/ReadableStream sources reproduce hidden
+cancellation failures, unstarted-generator leaks and unjoined pending reads.
+The whole scoped-consumption migration remains unimplemented; it does not require
+live model access to qualify.
+
+Manual compaction commands own action, result publication and recording settlement
+through cancellation. A completed checkpoint remains visible and cannot be
+reissued by later refusal. All renderers share one exhaustive outcome formatter;
+absent counts are refused and insufficient request room has its own status.
+Complete authored directives reach exact admission. The full ownership contract
+is described in [manual compaction commands](manual-compaction-commands.md).
