+# Manual compaction command ownership
+
+Both compression commands return one completed `ChatCompressionInfo` through a
+`compression` command result. The command has no consumer selection or UI side
+effects. A shared exhaustive formatter names every status and requires exact
+nonnegative integer counts. A smaller candidate that still leaves insufficient
+room for the next request has its own status. Failed attempts identify retained
+original history; a successful result describes the committed checkpoint, whose
+size is distinct from the subsequently restored startup context.
+
+The terminal stores the typed compression marker needed for rewind mapping. One
+factory adds its text projection for browser replay and validates the shared
+history type without erasing the record-compatible inferred object shape. Terminal resume validates
+completed counts through the same formatter. Pending work belongs to the command
+executor's processing state and carries no compression counts. External rewind
+admission and queued input respect that owner.
+
+The executor owns the action, result handling and recorder flush until they
+settle. Cancellation requests abort without releasing processing. Completed
+messages, compression results and Goal observations remain publishable after
+cancellation; a result requesting future work is refused. Confirmation
+continuations share the existing owner. Cancellation resolves pending
+confirmation without starting another action. A recording failure refuses any
+not-yet-submitted prompt, while already presented action facts remain visible.
+
+ACP already owns each prompt through its completion promise. It records and
+flushes the completed command projection, then delivers it; both obligations are
+attempted and their independent failures are preserved. Headless output emits the
+session-level compaction system record independently of an assistant message's
+lifetime, including when its adapter is reused across turns. All renderers use the
+same completed outcome.
+
+A checkpoint is recorded and flushed before live history installation. If later
+installation, telemetry or startup restoration fails, `CompactionFinalizationError`
+retains that operation's completed info and its independent cause. The client
+latches failed finalization and refuses further chat access until the session is
+restored with a new client. A later refusal never reissues the earlier completed
+result. Pre-admission telemetry failure retains the generated attempt as a named
+protocol failure with its observed output.
+
+Slash-command recording is queued admission, not a completion value. Synchronous
+construction or inactive-admission failures enter the recorder's existing
+persistent failure state, just like an asynchronous append failure. Its existing
+failure notification is separate from an already completed UI action; `flush`
+certifies settlement or rejects with the original failure. This preserves dialog
+and settings completion without inventing another writer or a strict-mode flag.
+
+User and hook compaction instructions reach the existing exact directive-size
+check in full. They are admitted as supplied or refused. No reasoning budget,
+output budget, compaction reserve or retention policy changes.
+
+Verification covers named outcomes, invalid counts, held cancellation and flush,
+refused successor admission, confirmation cancellation, finalization failure and
+subsequent refusal, replay projections, reused headless adapters, and complete
+directive admission. Static source contracts and exact delivery checks complement native TypeScript
+builds and CPU-only runtime tests. The shared formatter and held ACP recording
+and delivery controls execute against their actual package configurations;
+provider boundaries remain mocked.
