--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/design/session-writer-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@
+# Session writer ownership
+
+Every recording session owns an exclusive writer lease. Terminal, headless, ACP,
+and daemon consumers meet the same obligation through Config and the recorder.
+A read-only Config cannot dispatch generation because dispatch itself requires
+an active durable recorder.
+
+## Invariants
+
+1. One cooperating process owns a session writer under its fixed runtime base.
+2. The recorder is inactive until the owner has reloaded the transcript under
+   its lease. Preview data never selects the authoritative append parent.
+3. Every append verifies the owner token, transcript identity, and byte length.
+   Timestamp-only drift requires stable content verification.
+4. A durable append failure poisons subsequent writes and generation admission.
+5. Execution-directory changes leave persistence and artifact ownership fixed.
+6. Readers that feed model history refuse corrupt framing and incomplete active
+   history. Wire-stream partial observations are a separate format contract.
+
+## Lease protocol
+
+The lock is stored at:
+
+```text
+<runtime base>/tmp/session-writer-locks/<encoded session id>.lock
+```
+
+Its immutable record contains a random owner token, PID, host, process kind, acquisition time, Qwen version, and (when available) a stable OS process-start identity. Linux uses the kernel boot ID plus the process start ticks, so wall-clock corrections cannot make a live owner appear stale. Darwin normalizes the process-start probe to the C locale and UTC so two processes with different environments compare the same identity. The identity distinguishes PID reuse when the platform exposes it reliably. A foreign-host owner and any state whose safety cannot be proven fail closed.
+
+Acquisition creates a fully written temporary record and links it into the lock name atomically. A valid live owner returns `session_writer_conflict`. A valid dead local owner can be renamed, rechecked, and reclaimed. Reclaim guards form bounded owner generations so another process can recover if a reclaimer itself crashes. A malformed, symlink, or non-regular lock returns `session_writer_unavailable` rather than being guessed stale.
+
+The lease snapshots whether the transcript exists, its file identity, security metadata, byte length, and an in-memory incremental SHA-256 state. Existence, length, device/inode, mode, owner/group, and link-count changes fail closed. Birth, change, and modification timestamps are advisory: timestamp-only drift triggers a stable full-content check through one file handle and is accepted only when the digest is unchanged. Extended attributes and ACL entries that do not change the mode are not fingerprinted separately. When such an operation surfaces as timestamp drift, it is accepted after the same content check if all hard state remains unchanged; if the filesystem exposes no observed timestamp difference, the operation is not detected. `appendJsonLine` applies the same check after opening its append handle, advances a candidate digest with the known bytes, and commits the digest and expected state only after a successful durable append, post-write path verification, and final owner check. New transcript creation uses exclusive creation.
+
+Acquiring an existing transcript performs one O(n) streaming read to establish the digest baseline, using a buffer bounded to 1 MiB; ordinary appends remain incremental. A reconciliation scan requires timestamps to remain stable from its pre-read state through its post-read state and retries timestamp-only instability at most three times. This stability interval is necessary because a sequential digest can match the expected content even when a non-cooperating writer changes an already-read offset behind the read cursor. If timestamps continue changing, the lease returns `session_writer_unavailable` instead of accepting a potentially torn snapshot.
+
+The incremental digest is a live-process integrity check, not a persisted proof for certified handoff. A non-cooperating writer can still overwrite an equal-length prefix during an append without leaving a timestamp difference visible at one of this process's state observations. Closing that existing boundary would require an unconditional O(n) post-write scan, make repeated appends quadratic, and is outside the cooperative writer guarantee.
+
+## Activation, transitions, and close
+
+`Config.initialize()` acquires recording ownership before runtime initialization.
+It resolves active/archive state, reloads the transcript, verifies that snapshot,
+and activates the recorder. Initialization failure closes and releases it.
+
+`Config.startNewSession()` drains and closes the outgoing owner, acquires and
+reloads the incoming owner, and restores initialized model history before
+publishing the session. The same operation handles new and resumed sessions,
+including authoritative same-ID reloads. Failure restores the prior owner and
+history; rollback failure closes admission and preserves both errors. Shutdown
+races cannot reopen or publish a session. Turn and tool counters, goal state,
+and todo processing belong to the selected session.
+
+The recorder's write barrier serializes active-history reads and forks behind
+accepted writes. SessionService acquires the same lease for inactive-source
+forks, rename, archive/unarchive, and deletion. Callers already holding ownership
+pass that exact lease. A fork owns its destination before durable creation.
+Failure of the destination does not poison a still-valid source. Deletion waits
+for retained usage accounting to become durable.
+
+Shutdown attempts every independent cleanup obligation, waits for recorder
+settlement, and releases ownership even when another cleanup step fails. Exact
+owner checks prevent an older failed initialization from closing a newer entry.
+Managed certified handoff has additional trust and sealed-proof requirements,
+described in [Certified session writer handoff](certified-session-writer-handoff.md).
+
+## Error contract
+
+| Kind                         | JSON-RPC | HTTP | Meaning                                                 |
+| ---------------------------- | -------: | ---: | ------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `session_writer_conflict`    | `-32020` |  409 | Another live process owns the session.                  |
+| `session_writer_lost`        | `-32021` |  409 | This Config no longer owns its lock.                    |
+| `session_transcript_changed` | `-32022` |  409 | The JSONL changed outside the expected append sequence. |
+| `session_writer_unavailable` | `-32023` |  503 | Ownership could not be verified safely.                 |
+
+External responses use fixed messages and `errorKind`; they do not expose PID, host, owner token, lock path, or transcript path.
+
+A symlink or non-regular transcript path with no prior regular-file baseline is `session_writer_unavailable`. Once a lease has established a regular-file baseline, replacing that path with a symlink or another non-regular file is an external transcript replacement and is classified as `session_transcript_changed`.
+
+## Filesystem and trust boundary
+
+The runtime filesystem must support atomic same-directory hard links without
+replacement. Unsupported ownership operations fail unavailable. All writers
+must honor the lease; it cannot fence arbitrary external file mutations. No
+reader automatically repairs ambiguous transcript history.
+
+## Verification
+
+Local tests cover all nine terminal/headless/ACP contention directions, strict
+activation and appends, authoritative reload, new/resume/rollback transitions,
+shutdown races, fixed storage roots, source and destination fork ownership,
+mutation conflicts, durable failure poisoning, and preserved cleanup errors.
+Lock tests cover live and dead owners, crashed reclaimers, malformed locks,
+transcript replacement, timestamp reconciliation, UTF-8 byte lengths, and exact
+owner release. Platform-dependent process identity controls are reported with
+explicit skips when unavailable.
