+The dashboard presents Session, Activity and Efficiency views. Each view consumes
+canonical generation summaries produced by the accounting and history services.
+UI code does not own a second request or token accumulator.
 
-**Goal:** Replace the Overview and Models tabs in the `/stats` TUI with an Activity tab (time-based trends) and an Efficiency tab (performance metrics and tool analysis).
+## Data ownership
 
-**Architecture:** Extend the data layer (`usageHistoryService`, `statsDataService`) with delta calculation, tool duration, and latency fields. Replace the two UI tab components in `StatsDialog.tsx`. Change the heatmap from session-count to token-based with today highlight.
+`SessionMetrics.generationUsage` describes the live session. Model and source
+buckets carry the same `GenerationUsageSummary` shape. Each summary distinguishes
+requests, served reports, unfinalized requests, and finalized requests without
+served usage. Its nullable usage contains the five served counts. Zero is a
+reported count; null means no served report is available.
 
-**Tech Stack:** TypeScript, Ink/React, Vitest, braille ASCII charts
+`metricsToUsageRecord` retains model summaries and their actual latency, tool
+activity and duration, and file changes. `aggregateUsage` combines canonical
+summaries for the whole report, models and projects. Missing retained tool
+duration remains null. Safe-integer overflow refuses aggregation.
 
----
+`statsDataService` replaces a persisted current-session record with its live
+snapshot before aggregation. Its `daily` points come from `summarizeUsageDays`,
+the same grouping operation used by the web dashboard. Each point retains its
+date, sessions, tools, file changes and generation summary. Display code reads
+that summary directly.
 
